<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lucien Murat</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1803">1803</a>–<a href="/years/1878">1878</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Napoléon Lucien Charles Murat</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lucien Murat was the second son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joachim-murat">Joachim Murat</a>, King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, and Caroline Bonaparte, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s sister. In 1812 Napoleon granted him the principality of Pontecorvo after Bernadotte gave it up, but he lost it at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> in 1815. He lived for years in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and later became a politician in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> under his cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lucien-murat.jpg" alt="Lucien Murat" width="320" height="259" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: K.A · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tablier_V%C3%A9n%C3%A9rable_du_Rite_Fran%C3%A7ais_moderne.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Lucien Charles Joseph Napoléon Murat was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> on 16 May 1803, the second of the four children of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joachim-murat">Joachim Murat</a> and Caroline Bonaparte; his siblings were Achille, Marie Letizia and Louise Julie. Through his mother his uncles included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-bonaparte">Joseph Bonaparte</a>, Napoleon I, Lucien, Louis and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerome-bonaparte">Jérôme Bonaparte</a>, while his father's parents were Pierre Murat-Jordy, an innkeeper and postmaster, and Jeanne Loubières. After his father's execution, ordered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-i-of-the-two-sicilies">Ferdinand IV of Naples</a>, he had to flee the Italian Peninsula, and from 1815 to 1822 he and Achille were educated at Schloss Frohsdorf in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Pursued by the Austrian authorities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, he set out for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, where Achille had settled, but was shipwrecked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and held for months until Achille obtained the help of President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-monroe">James Monroe</a>. He reached the United States in April 1825, met his uncle Joseph in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> and travelled widely in the West, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>. On 18 August 1831 he married Caroline Georgina Fraser, a former governess in Joseph's household at Bordentown, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-jersey">New Jersey</a>, and after years of money troubles the couple opened a girls' boarding school.</p><p>In 1838 and 1844 he explored a claim to his family's throne. After the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-philippe-i">Louis-Philippe</a> in 1848 he returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, was elected to the Constituent Assembly, became minister to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a> in 1849 and received the rank of senator and the title of prince in 1852. After the coup of 2 December 1851 the Grand Orient de France made him its Grand Master; he passed its constitution of 1854 and bought its building at 16 rue Cadet. His manifesto of 1861 claiming the throne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> displeased his cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a>, and he gave up the claim.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After the surrender at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a> in 1870 he was imprisoned with Marshal François Achille Bazaine, and after the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">Second Empire</a> he spent a short time in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, where he had grist mills and saw mills near Evans Mills, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 10 April 1878, and his wife on 10 February 1879. Their son Joachim became the 4th Prince Murat, and the actor René Auberjonois was among their descendants.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lucien, 3rd Prince Murat” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1810 he was elected crown prince of Sweden and gave up the principality, which Napoleon in 1812 granted to <b>Lucien Murat</b>, son of King Joachim Murat of the Kingdom of Naples (1806–1815).</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-pontecorvo">Principality of Pontecorvo</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.47,9.19&amp;year=1851" aria-label="Milan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4768 560 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Lucien Murat"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5218 830h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5218 830h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.47,9.19&amp;year=1851">What was here in 1851? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
